diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index ff92be649ff3f610b38798b3310751534d032814..46e2ae7cf1c5d05db2ed767c6a4953758e08b9be 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -3,3 +3,61 @@
 A small TypeScript library, with no runtime dependency, of building blocks for our services that store,
 exchange and update JSON: configuration files, API payloads and stored records. It is imported by the
 other projects of the team, so its behaviour must follow the published standards it implements.
+
+## Standards implemented
+
+| Standard                                                 | API                 |
+| -------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------- |
+| [RFC 6901](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6901) — JSON Pointer | `resolvePointer()` |
+
+## JSON Pointer — `resolvePointer(document, pointer)`
+
+Returns the value that `pointer` refers to in `document`.
+
+```ts
+import { resolvePointer } from 'json-toolkit';
+
+const document = { users: [{ name: 'Ada' }, { name: 'Linus' }], 'a/b': true };
+
+resolvePointer(document, '/users/0/name'); // 'Ada'
+resolvePointer(document, '/a~1b'); //        true
+resolvePointer(document, ''); //             the whole document
+```
+
+A pointer is a sequence of reference tokens, each prefixed by `/`. Within a token, `~1` stands for `/`
+and `~0` for `~`; `~1` is unescaped first, so `~01` denotes the member named `~1`. The empty pointer
+`''` refers to the document itself, and `'/'` refers to the member whose name is the empty string.
+
+Each token is read as an object member name, or — when the value at hand is an array — as an array
+index: `0`, or a digit string with no leading zero.
+
+### Errors
+
+`resolvePointer` throws a `JsonPointerError` rather than returning `undefined`, so that a member whose
+value is `null`, `false`, `0` or `''` is not confused with a member that is absent. The error's `code`
+says which of the two contracts was broken:
+
+| `code`             | Cause                                                       | Examples                                |
+| ------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------- |
+| `'invalid-pointer'` | The pointer is not a valid JSON Pointer per RFC 6901.      | `'users'`, `'/~'`, `'/~2'`, `42`        |
+| `'unresolved'`      | The pointer is well-formed, but names no value.            | an absent member, an out-of-range index, an index on a non-array, any token below a primitive or `null` |
+
+The error also carries the offending `pointer`, as a string, or `undefined` when the argument was not
+a string at all.
+
+Two cases are worth calling out, because both are deliberate:
+
+- Only _own_ members resolve. `'/__proto__'` and `'/toString'` are `'unresolved'` against `{}`; they
+  never reach the prototype chain.
+- `'-'`, the RFC 6901 token for the position just past the last array element, is always `'unresolved'`:
+  no value is stored there. (Operations that _add_ to an array, where `'-'` is meaningful, will handle
+  it themselves.)
+
+## Development
+
+```sh
+npm install
+npm test        # node:test, run straight on the TypeScript sources
+npm run typecheck
+npm run build   # emits dist/
+```
