@@ -0,0 +1,61 @@
+---
+id: dl-001-strict-rfc-6901-conformance-and-a-typed-jsonpointererror
+type: decision-log
+title: "Strict RFC 6901 conformance and a typed JsonPointerError"
+status: approved
+---
+
+# Strict RFC 6901 conformance and a typed JsonPointerError
+
+## Context
+
+`resolvePointer(document, pointer)` must return the value a JSON Pointer refers to, and throw when the
+pointer is invalid or does not resolve. RFC 6901 leaves a library two things to settle.
+
+First, how strict to be. The specification's grammar accepts more than the obvious cases (the empty
+pointer, a trailing `/`, an empty member name) and rejects more than the obvious cases (a dangling
+`~`, an array index with a leading zero, the `-` token). Many implementations are loose here, which
+makes them disagree with each other. json-toolkit is imported by the other projects of the team, so a
+pointer that resolves in one service must resolve in all of them: the README states that behaviour
+follows the published standard.
+
+Second, how to signal failure. `undefined` is not available as a sentinel: a JSON document may hold
+`null`, `false`, `0` or `''`, and "the member is absent" must not be confused with "the member is
+there and falsy". So failure has to be an exception — but callers still need to tell a caller's bug (a
+malformed pointer, often a hard-coded constant) apart from a data mismatch (a well-formed pointer
+against a document that does not answer it), because the first is a defect and the second is routine.
+
+## Decision
+
+Follow the RFC 6901 grammar exactly, in both directions.
+
+- A pointer is valid iff it is a string that is empty or starts with `/`, and every `~` in it is
+  followed by `0` or `1`. This is equivalent to the ABNF of section 3 for a JavaScript string: `/`
+  cannot appear inside a token, so splitting on `/` recovers the tokens, and no character is
+  forbidden outright. It is checked with two linear tests rather than one nested-quantifier regular
+  expression, so a pointer arriving in an API payload cannot cause runaway backtracking.
+- Unescape `~1` before `~0`, as section 4 requires, so `~01` denotes the member named `~1`.
+- An array token must match `0|[1-9][0-9]*`. `01`, `+1`, `-1`, `1.0` and `length` name nothing in an
+  array; `-` is well-formed but never resolves, since no value is stored past the last element.
+- Read own properties only, so `/__proto__` and `/toString` do not resolve against `{}`.
+
+Throw a `JsonPointerError` carrying a `code` of `'invalid-pointer'` or `'unresolved'`, and the
+offending `pointer`. `pointer` is `undefined`, not `''`, when the argument was not a string, because
+`''` is itself a valid pointer — the whole document — and must not be reported as the cause.
+
+Type the document as an exported `JsonValue` union, and validate each node as the traversal reaches
+it rather than deep-validating the whole document up front: that keeps resolution proportional to the
+pointer's length instead of the document's size, and every node a pointer actually touches is still
+checked.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The two error codes are part of the published contract. Callers branch on them; later features reuse
+  them instead of inventing their own vocabulary.
+- Strictness will reject pointers that looser libraries accept — `'/foo/01'`, `'/foo/-'`,
+  `'users/0'`. That is the point, but it is a migration hazard for a service moving onto this library,
+  so the README lists these cases explicitly.
+- `-` is rejected here on purpose. Operations that *add* to an array, where `-` does mean something,
+  will have to give it meaning themselves rather than inherit it from resolution.
+- The `JsonValue` union is now public, so widening it later (to permit `undefined`, say) would be a
+  breaking change.
