@@ -0,0 +1,49 @@
+---
+id: dl-002-split-the-pointer-module-from-the-package-entry-point-for-reuse
+type: decision-log
+title: "Split the pointer module from the package entry point for reuse"
+status: approved
+---
+
+# Split the pointer module from the package entry point for reuse
+
+## Context
+
+JSON Pointer is the first of several features that build on each other; the next ones will use it. The
+request names only one export, `resolvePointer` from `src/index.ts`. Putting the implementation there
+would make the entry point grow into the whole library, and would leave the next features with only
+the public API to build on — yet they need more than it offers. An operation that *writes* through a
+pointer, for instance, needs the parsed tokens so it can reach a value's parent and its last key;
+`resolvePointer` only hands back the value itself.
+
+The repository also had no way to type-check or build: `tsconfig.json` declared `declaration` and
+`outDir` but no compiler was installed, and `node --test` had never been run against a TypeScript
+source.
+
+## Decision
+
+Put the implementation in `src/json-pointer.ts` and keep `src/index.ts` as a re-export surface only.
+Inside the module, split parsing from evaluation: `parsePointer` validates a pointer and returns its
+unescaped tokens, and `resolvePointer` walks them. `parsePointer` is exported from the module but not
+from `index.ts`, so the next features can share one definition of "a valid pointer" without that
+becoming a published API we have to keep.
+
+Run the tests straight on the TypeScript sources with Node's type stripping — no build step, no test
+framework, keeping the project free of a runtime dependency. This needs relative imports to name the
+`.ts` file they resolve to, since Node does not remap `.js` to `.ts`, so `tsconfig.json` gains
+`allowImportingTsExtensions` and `rewriteRelativeImportExtensions`: `tsc` rewrites those specifiers
+to `.js` when it emits `dist/`. Tests live in `test/`, outside the `include` of the build config, and
+`tsconfig.test.json` type-checks them without emitting.
+
+Add `typescript` and `@types/node` as dev dependencies, with `npm run typecheck` and `npm run build`.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- `index.ts` stays a readable inventory of what the package offers; the standards it implements each
+  get their own module.
+- `parsePointer` is internal, so its shape can change while the features that share it change with it.
+  If a consumer eventually needs it, promoting it is a deliberate step.
+- Sources must keep writing `.ts` in relative import specifiers. A `.js` specifier would type-check
+  but fail at runtime under `node --test`, which is the opposite of the usual NodeNext habit and will
+  surprise someone.
+- The build depends on `rewriteRelativeImportExtensions`, which needs TypeScript 5.7 or later.
