+---
+id: task-001-implement-rfc-6901-json-pointer-resolution
+type: task
+title: "Implement RFC 6901 JSON Pointer resolution"
+status: approved
+---
+
+# Implement RFC 6901 JSON Pointer resolution
+
+## Request
+
+Give the services a standard way to refer to a value inside a JSON document: implement JSON Pointer
+per RFC 6901, exporting `resolvePointer(document, pointer)` from `src/index.ts`. It returns the value
+the pointer refers to, and throws when the pointer is not a valid JSON Pointer or does not resolve to
+a value of the document. The first of several features that build on each other.
+
+## What was done
+
+Tests first, then the implementation, as the testing directive requires. Every acceptance criterion
+was new behaviour, so all of it was red-first: `test/json-pointer.test.ts` was written and run against
+a missing module before `src/` existed.
+
+- `src/json-pointer.ts` — `parsePointer` (validation + unescaping) and `resolvePointer` (evaluation),
+  plus `JsonPointerError`, `JsonPointerErrorCode`, `JsonValue` and `JsonObject`.
+- `src/index.ts` — the package's public surface: `resolvePointer`, `JsonPointerError` and the types.
+  `parsePointer` stays internal, for the features that come next to share.
+- `test/json-pointer.test.ts` — 20 `node:test` tests.
+- `README.md` — a section on the API, its escaping and index rules, and its two error codes.
+- `tsconfig.json`, `tsconfig.test.json`, `package.json` — type-checking and a build (see dl-002).
+- `.wingfoil/dna.yaml` — project name and description, the `json-pointer` module, the source, test and
+  doc paths, and the TypeScript/Node/`node:test` stack, all recorded via `wingfoil dna`.
+
+## Coverage
+
+The project declares no coverage threshold, so the testing directive's "happy path plus an edge or
+error path per behaviour" was the bar. Both are covered for each behaviour:
+
+- Resolution: all twelve pointers of the RFC 6901 section 5 example document; nesting; the empty key,
+  once and twice; `~01` and `~0~1`; a whole-document pointer against an object, an array, a string and
+  `null`.
+- Falsy members: `null`, `false`, `0` and `''` are returned, not reported absent.
+- Malformed pointers (`'invalid-pointer'`): no leading `/`, a dangling `~`, `~2`, `~x`, `~~0`, and a
+  pointer that is not a string at all.
+- Unresolvable pointers (`'unresolved'`): an absent member; inherited properties (`__proto__`,
+  `constructor`, `toString`, `hasOwnProperty`) and an own member shadowing one; non-index array tokens
+  (`01`, `+1`, `-1`, `1.0`, `' 1'`, `1e0`, `length`, `''`); an out-of-range index; `-`; and any token
+  below a string, number, boolean or `null`.
+- Scoping: the array index rules apply only to arrays, so an object member may be named `0`, `01`,
+  `-` or `length` and still resolve.
+- The error carries the pointer, and the document is left untouched.
+
+## Verification
+
+- `npm test` — 20 tests, 20 pass, 0 fail.
+- `npm run typecheck` — clean, `strict`, sources and tests.
+- `npm run build` — emits `dist/`, with the `.ts` import specifiers rewritten to `.js`.
+- A throwaway consumer package, resolving `json-toolkit` through `main`/`types`, type-checks and runs:
+  `JsonValue` and `JsonPointerErrorCode` arrive as real types, not `any`. Worth noting that `tsc`
+  leaves the `.ts` specifier in the emitted `.d.ts`; TypeScript resolves it to the sibling `.d.ts`, so
+  consumers are unaffected, but it looks wrong on inspection.
+
+## Decisions recorded
+
+- `dl-001` — strict RFC 6901 conformance and a typed `JsonPointerError`.
+- `dl-002` — splitting the pointer module from the package entry point for reuse.
+
+## For the approver
+
+Both decision-logs are submitted and await approval; `dl-001` fixes the published error contract that
+the next features will inherit, so it is worth a look before they are built.
